{"name":"Cadillac Coupe","hex":"#c0362c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"coupe","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shallow glass or glass dish, usually with a stem, in which sparkling wine or desserts are served.","An ice cream dessert served in a coupe glass."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A two-seater car, normally a sports car. (variant of coupé)","A car with two doors and a nonconvertible roof. (variant of coupé)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/coupe"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cadillac","blocks":[{"text":"Cadillac Motor Car Division, or simply Cadillac, is the luxury vehicle division of the American automobile manufacturer General Motors (GM). Its major markets are the United States, Canada, and China; Cadillac models are also distributed in 34 additional markets worldwide. Historically, Cadillac automobiles were at the top of the luxury field within the United States, often competing with Lincoln, but have been outsold by European luxury brands including BMW and Mercedes since the 2000s. In 2019, Cadillac sold 390,458 vehicles worldwide, a record for the brand."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/6/66/Cadillac_logo_BW.svg/330px-Cadillac_logo_BW.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cadillac","wikidata":"Q27436"},{"title":"Coupe","blocks":[{"text":"A coupe or coupé is a passenger car with a sloping or truncated rear roofline and typically with two doors."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/2019_Audi_TT_Sport_40_TFSi_S-A_2.0_Rear.jpg/330px-2019_Audi_TT_Sport_40_TFSi_S-A_2.0_Rear.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coupe","wikidata":"Q213853"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Cadillac_Model_A_1902_%286829591487%29.jpg/250px-Cadillac_Model_A_1902_%286829591487%2